Also check the wrap-around line wrap logic in exportDocToPdf — I wrote a convoluted carry mechanism that mutates tokens... Let me re-examine:

```js
for(const t of tokens){...
 if(lineW+tw>W&&line.length&&t.word.trim()!==''){
   const carry=t.word;t.word='';
   await flush();          // flush draws current line
   listFirst=false;line=[];lineW=0;
   tokens.unshift({...t,word:carry,});   // push copy with carry
   const again=tokens.shift();          // shift... unshift then shift = same copy? 
```
unshift adds copy at front; shift removes front = that copy. But the for...of loop is iterating tokens — mutating while iterating is messy. This wrap approach is fragile. Rewrite line-breaking cleanly with an index-based loop:

```js
let lines=[];let line=[],lineW=0;
for(const t of tokens){
  const f=await getFont(t.fam,t.bold,t.italic);
  if(t.word.trim()===''&&line.length===0)continue;   // skip leading spaces
  const tw=f.widthOfTextAtSize(t.word,t.pt);t.w=tw;
  if(lineW+tw>W&&line.length&&t.word.trim()!==''){
    if(line[line.length-1].word.trim()==='')line.pop(); // drop trailing space
    lines.push(line);line=[];lineW=0;
    if(t.word.trim()==='')continue;
  }
  line.push(t);lineW+=tw;
}
if(line.length)lines.push(line);
```
Then for each line (with listFirst only for first line): compute flush with its own fonts per run. Since each token stores its own fam/bold/italic/color/pt, flush draws per token with its font. lineSize = max pt in line.

Rewrite flush as a function drawing a given line:

```js
