Interesting — with a REAL mouse click, tables ARE inserted in all cases. So the app works in current Chrome headless. But wait — look at CASE1 dom: `TABLE,P,H1,P` — the table was inserted BEFORE the H1! That's odd placement (caret was at start?). But inserted nonetheless.

Hmm wait, dom order is weird: TABLE,P,H1,P — but the default content is `<h1>Judul dokumen</h1><p>Mulai menulis...` — the fresh page isn't empty; it has seeded content ("Judul dokumen" heading + hint paragraph). Hmm, earlier in this session the fresh page had that placeholder. But in the older test suite the fresh page had empty `<p></p>`... Actually in the older suite I always SET innerHTML first, so I never saw the initial content. So the fresh doc has placeholder text (probably seeded by initDocBuilder for guidance).

OK so real clicks work in headless Chrome 157. But the USER says the table doesn't appear. Possibilities:
1. User is testing the DEPLOYED site with old code (before the table feature, or an older app.js cached). Very likely! They said "di saya" — they probably deployed/pulled earlier. Their site may serve cached app.js or an old deployment.
2. User's browser is older (e.g., Safari/Firefox) where execCommand or the flow fails differently.
3. Real bug in an edge case: e.g., user clicks ▦ while NO selection and document never focused... covered (fallback appends). What about when the user has MULTIPLE pages and caret on page 2? Fine.

Hmm wait — there might be one more real-world failure mode: `document.execCommand('insertHTML')` inserts the table INSIDE an empty `<p>` when caret is at start of a non-empty paragraph... In CASE1, dom shows TABLE,P,H1,P — table placed BEFORE h1? The caret at openDocPage focuses the first page → caret at start of... the H1? insertHTML placed the table before the H1. Works.

But hold on — maybe there IS a genuine cross-browser problem: `document.execCommand('insertHTML', ...)` with a `<table>` when the caret is INSIDE an empty `<p></p>`: Chrome inserts table inside the <p> → invalid; Chrome then... it might DROP the table? Chrome hoists it out. Firefox might refuse.

The user's environment: unknown browser. The safest fix remains: **stop relying on execCommand for block insertion; use DOM manipulation directly.** This makes table insertion deterministic across browsers and focus states:

- Compute insertion position:
  - If docLastRange (or current selection) is inside a page: find the block element (p/h1/h2/h3/h4/li/table/img/div... top-level child of page) containing the caret.
