239: 
240: Every method except `info()` takes `onProgress`, and so does `run()`. qpdf calls it while it **writes** the output:
241: 
242: - Values are integers from 0 to 100 that strictly increase within one output file (linearize's first value can be
243:   above 0). Each new value is passed on at once, never batched (at most 101 calls per file).
244: - There are no calls before qpdf starts writing (reading the input, copying pages). For PDFs with many small objects
245:   that can be most of the job, so show an indeterminate state until the first call.
246: - 100% means qpdf finished writing that output, not that your work is done. If you run more qpdf calls afterwards
247:   (or before, like a password check), show a short "Finishing…" state instead of a bar stuck at 100%.
248: - `split()` starts again at 0 for each output file.
249: - `run()` reports progress only for commands that write a PDF; `--check`, `--json`, a sole help option such as
250:   `--version`, and similar never call it.
251: - No calls after the promise settles or after `terminate()`. If the callback throws, the job continues and the
252:   error is rethrown asynchronously (in Node, as an uncaught exception).
253: - With `inline: true` (the default in Node), qpdf runs on the calling thread: a page can't repaint between calls, and
254:   async work the callback starts (such as sending to a socket) runs only after the job ends. For live progress in
255:   Node, run qpdf in a `worker_threads` worker.
256: - To detect a stuck job, keep a size-based time limit until the first call, then fail only when no call has
257:   arrived for a while (we suggest 30 s: one percent can take seconds on a phone).
258: - Calls for a job keep coming until it settles, even if your UI has moved on (a job can't be cancelled, only the whole
259:   instance `terminate()`d). Ignore calls from a job you no longer show, for example by comparing a job counter.
